From: davidc@cs.uwa.edu.au (David Cake)
Subject: PSYCH:Myer-Briggs Nonsense

> > Can you point to a case where nearly identical companies with virtually
> > similar business conditions and competentcy of personal where the company
> > that used MB test did significantly better than the one that didn't?
>
Sounds a bit of a tall order to me - even for something that does have 
truth beyond doubt. Certainly doesn't seem to be a necessary condition to 
prove anything apart from overall business strategy under a particular set
of conditions. A scientific control based test is, I am afraid, not 
considered a practical methodology in almost any social science, or most
research of things social or psychological. To demand it here seems a little
unreasonable. Perhaps the dreaded hyper-rationalism rears its head?

The MB test is not a particularly controversial theory in terms of 
psychology. The only thing most psychologists have against it is that it
has very little clinical use (partly because it is designed more for use
on healthy people), and little predictive power outside the area of 
personality traits (ie can't tell you whos just bluffing, and whos a real
killer, for example). Its validity can be simply shown by high statistical 
correlations between patterns of answers and personality traits. It is 
really just a simple classification scheme for personality, and MB theory
is pretty vague - really just a description of the various types, and 
generalisations of relationships between people with these types. That\
doesn't make it any less valid, just limited - and its not really intended
as a clinical too for treatment of problems, more an aid to self 
understanding and understanding of relationships among the basically healthy.
	Unlike pseudosciences like astrology, people will reject most 
incorrect personality descriptions given by the MB. Eg people who know
little about astrology can not reliably choose between the description of 
their own star signs personality description and another. On the other hand,
people can reliably choose their MB classifications from others -especially
when they differ by more than one category.
	And finally, a note of synchronicity. About two hours before I read
this message I spoke to my father, who is currently employed at a 
psychiatric hospital, on the phone. HE asked me could I please return his
book on the Myers-Briggs test because he has to give a seminar on using
it in the mental health field to some of his co-workers. So perhaps I could
ask him to get some feedback from the psychiatrists and clinical
psychologists in his group?
							DAve Cake
