Date: Wed, 17 Mar 93 09:42:21 GMT
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: ECON/LAW: Tragedy of the commons

David Lubkin writes:
>If the purpose of the copyright is to protect the property rights of
>the author, I'd say that there should be NO time limit to copyright
>whatsoever.

Why?

>And while it is true that most copyrightable material dies a quiet death
>shortly after publication, publication rights for some works remain
>of value centuries or millenia after the author's death, e.g., 
>the writings of Aristotle, Chaucer, Lao-Tse, and Perry Metzger.

Who would own the writings of Aristotle, Chaucer, and Lao-Tse today if such
perpetual rights were in force?  (I leave aside the more complicated
question of whether entities such as Perry's deanimated, uploaded,
multiplied, or radically modified selves would continue to own his postings
here in a hundred years time, since the Singularity will be along any
century now to render the matter moot.)  Presumably the copyright would be
bought and sold down the ages, like a manuscript copy or any physical
commodity might be?

What purpose would this serve?  That such an object, were it to exist,
would be a valuable piece of property does not seem relevant to me.  Unlike
unowned physical objects, where an argument can be made that contention for
their use is resolved by the creation of property rights, there can be no
such contention over unowned information.  Anyone who wants can get a copy.
 For physical objects, property rights also create an incentive for the
owner not to let the property deteriorate.  This too does not apply to
information; in fact, if a few people have exclusive ownership of a piece
of information, it is more likely to be lost than if it is available to
all.  (If only the flying yogis of the past hadn't been so secretive!)

Property rights are not rights over things.  They are rights over people,
rights to prevent anyone but the owner from using the property.  For a
particular notion of property to be instituted, some positive argument in
favour must be made, since as I remarked in another posting, such rights do
not exist objectively, but only to serve some social purpose.

A case for intellectual property rights might be that for the creator of an
intellectual object to profit from it by selling copies, he must be able to
prevent unauthorised copying and use.  The enforcement of intellectual
property thus gives him a greater incentive for creating the original
object.  Thus more such objects are created, and everyone, even the people
who do not create such objects, gains more than they lose from other
people's property rights.  Whether that is a conclusive argument I am not
sure.  However, I do not see that it gives grounds for anyone but the
creator of a piece of information owning it, or for perpetual rights that
outlive the creator.

