Oddly enough, the only dreams that I am able to remember are lucid
dreams.  And I quite often have them.  No training... no conscious
attempts at training... I just can't dream any other way and recall
it when I wake up.

I haven't even read any books on lucid dreaming... so how do I
know that I'm really lucid dreaming?  I was taking a psych class, 
and the Professor was analyzing one of my dreams.  Actually, he 
said that he couldn't analyze it like a dream, because it wasn't.
This kind of pissed me off a bit... 'cause he said it was a fantasy,
and I had pointedly written the entire dream down that morning...
right after waking up... a six page dream.  He said that the dream 
was too complex and abstract not to be lucid.

When I was a kid, I used to lay down before going to sleep, and 
decide which of my old dreams I wanted to have.  Or, I would decide
to continue a dream from the night before.  It didn't always work,
but it usually did.  I had one dream in a trilogy... wierd.

Now, I find myself researching stuff in my dreams, and reading 
a lot.  I'm trying to get to the point where I remember my discoveries
on awaking... so that I can write them down... but so far, no luck.

It is also kind of cool in that I get to act out fantasies.  Be the
star of the show.  Quite often I switch identities in my dreams.  I'll
be the protagonist, antagonist, and side-kick all in one.  It can make
for an odd night.

How is this Extropian?  Simple, I'm reaching out into my entropic 
subconsious and gaining control and understanding of it.  If I can
figure out how to make my sleeping hours more productive... it is 
quite probably that my waking hours will be too.

Could someone please try to give me an analysis of the above ramblings?
Hints on how to go further?  Explanations of what I'm alread doing?
Does anyone else often switch identities in dreams?  Why do I do this?

