Date: Wed, 17 Mar 93 08:28:49 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: POLI: Source of income?!

Today I heard on the news that Cook County (the county in which Chicago
sits) has added sexual orientation to its laundry list of other traits for
which discrimination in housing, credit, government benefits, etc. is
forbidden.  In federal regulations, this sort of list usually includes: 
race, color, religion, sex, (middle or old) age, disability, veteran's
status, and country of origin.  Such lists more and more often include
sexual orientation.  

I like to remember all these traits by imagining a single individual who
has them all:  a black (race and color) Rastafarian (religion) woman (sex)
who is 40 or older (age) and wheelchair bound (disability) as a result of
the service she gave to the military (vetern's status) after moving to the
US from Jamaica (country of origin).  To keep her up to date with more
recent lists of protected classes, I imagine that this woman is also
lesbian (sexual orientation).

I can also remember all of these traits by imagining an individual who has
no traits that are protected, or at least that are not the sorts of traits
legislators really meant to protect: a white nonreligious man who is
younger than 40, not disabled, not a veteran, was born in the US, and
straight.

Anyhow, the reason I bring all this up is that today's news reported that
the Cook County protected status list includes a trait I have never heard
included before:  source of income.  I've never seen this in federal
statutes.  Can anyone tell me what sort of discrimination it is intended to
prevent?  How will I have to revise my models?  Will the omni-protected
woman have to start work as a garbage collector or prostitute?  Or just
quit work?  Will the anti-protected man have to become a doctor or lawyer? 
Or just take a job?

This sort of provision really seems to invite litigation.  Source of income
is a good proxy for many other traits that employers and credit agencies
can (so far) legally take into account.  It tells them a lot about
employment experience and income security, for example.

