From: lubkin@apollo.hp.com
Subject: ECON/LAW: Copyrights

I wrote:

    >If the purpose of the copyright is to protect the property rights of
    >the author, I'd say that there should be NO time limit to copyright
    >whatsoever.

Richard Kennaway asked how and why.

The wealth associated with a property is a weighted sum of its material
content and its information content.  The worth of a property can be
increased by increasing either aspect.  A mountain is worth more than
a molehill.  A t-shirt is worth more than raw cotton. 

[I'm omitting a lot of irrelevant side issues to focus on what I'm
trying to say.  Don't tell me that value is relative, or dependent on
context, for example.]

Intellectual property is just an end case, where the material content
is zero.

Why should it be protected?  For all the usual reasons that any property
is protected.  My basic right to own and control the fruits of my labor.  
And the very practical reason that if you do not allow people to own what 
they produce, they will produce less than if you do.

And the right should no more expire than your ownership of land should
expire.

Richard argued that perhaps the right should vest exclusively with the
creator, and should not be transferrable.  What is the social utility in
making property rights transferrable?  It increases the incentive for
people to create new wealth.  A property is worth more if its property
rights are transferrable.  Many people are motivated by the desire to
create wealth that they can transfer to their offspring, spouses, or
charitable organizations.

I don't buy the argument that you are not diminished by someone else
making a copy of your work, so therefore it should be okay.  Let's 
suppose I own a weekend cabin with a lake.  I am not diminished by your
swimming in my lake during the week.  Nonetheless, I may forbid you 
from using my property in that way.


-- David Lubkin.

