From: LEVY%LENNY@Venus.YCC.Yale.Edu
Subject: PSYCH: Myers-Briggs - personal testimony

A psychologist friend of mine recently had me take a version of the
Myers-Briggs test when I told her that I was having trouble deciding
what to do (stay in school, or go to work full-time).  I was very
skeptical before taking the test, became even more so when I read
the questions, which seemed simplistic and predictable, but finsihed
the test anyway out of respect for my friend.  To my surprise, the
characteristics attributed to the personality class the test put me
in were right on target.  Just to put on my asbestos long-johns, I'll
admit that it's entirely possible that this was a "horoscope" effect,
but the other personality types were very different (in my view at
least) from what I think of as my own personality.  More practially,
I later read that my chosen profession (computer programming) has
a disproportionately large percentage of persons of my personality
type (INTJ).

In retrospect, I don't think there's anything mystical about the 
Myers-Briggs test; like many other tools in psychology, it's designed
to reduce a high-dimensional representation (answers to many questions)
to a low-dimensional representation (a few personality types), so that
the person taking it doesn't have to sit for hours pondering small
details.  Finally, I'd like to add that if I had taken this test
six years ago, I would probably would have avoided a great deal
of time-wasting and gone directly to the career that I ended up with
(which was one of five or six listed under INTJ).

-- Simon

